
[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more question before I lay out approaches.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether tracking covers only successful logins or also failures changes the module's contract, because a
  failed login has no [38;5;153muserId[39m to report — the whole point of the identifier you chose. Supporting failures means the
  event schema has a nullable identity and the module can't assume one. That's a data-model decision, awkward to
  retrofit once events are being emitted and consumed.

  - [1mSuccesses only[0m — buys you the simplest schema: every event has a real [38;5;153muserId[39m, no null cases, no branching. Costs:
    you learn nothing about failed attempts, which is usually the signal people actually want from login tracking.
    Adding failures later means widening the schema and revisiting any consumer.
  - [1mSuccesses and failures[0m — buys you the security-relevant half (repeated failures, unknown usernames) and matches what
    "track who logged in" tends to mean in practice. Costs: identity is nullable, you need a failure-reason field, and
    you must be careful never to put the submitted password anywhere near an event.
  - [1mEvery attempt, including client-side validation rejects[0m — also captures the [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m bail at [38;5;153mapp.js:26[39m. Buys you
    complete funnel visibility. Costs: noisy events that mostly reflect typos, and a third event shape.

  [1mMy recommendation: successes and failures, excluding validation rejects.[0m The nullable identity is a small cost paid
  once at design time, and a tracker blind to failed logins answers the least interesting version of your question.
  Validation rejects never reached auth, so they belong to form analytics, not login tracking.

